      "id": 1,
      "text": "Windsor was the final stop on the Underground Railroad, where escaped slaves found refuge before crossing into Canada.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        6,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windsor%2C_Ontario"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s name changed from la Petite Côte to Windsor, after the English town in Berkshire, in the mid‑1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windsor%2C_Ontario"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During Prohibition, Windsor’s rum‑running scene attracted Al Capone and his “Purple Gang,” who smuggled liquor across the border.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.uwindsor.ca/history/332/history-windsor"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The François Baby House, built in 1812, now houses Windsor’s Community Museum and preserves the city’s War of 1812 history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windsor%2C_Ontario"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Windsor’s Sandwich neighbourhood hosts Mackenzie Hall, the former Essex County Courthouse from 1855, one of the city’s oldest buildings.",
